 ## Overview
-Point-NeRF models a radiance field using a neural 3D point cloud, where each point carries a latent feature vector, rather than a dense voxel grid or a single global MLP. It renders efficiently by aggregating these neural point features near scene surfaces within a ray-marching pipeline. It is distinguished from standard NeRF by its explicit geometric initialization via multi-view stereo (MVS) and its significantly faster training convergence, achieving comparable visual quality with approximately 30× faster training time [S1, unofficial].
+Point-NeRF models a radiance field using a neural 3D point cloud, where each point carries a latent feature vector, rather than a continuous volumetric function [S1, unofficial]. It renders efficiently by aggregating neural point features near scene surfaces within a ray marching-based pipeline [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from standard NeRF by its discrete, point-based representation, which allows for initialization via pre-trained deep networks (e.g., MVSNet) and achieves approximately 30× faster training times while surpassing the visual quality of standard NeRF [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-Point-NeRF replaces the implicit coordinate-to-color/density mapping of standard NeRF with a sparse set of learnable 3D points, each associated with a high-dimensional latent code. The rendering process remains volume integration along rays, but the density $\sigma$ and color $\mathbf{c}$ are derived from the features of the nearest points to the ray sample location.
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Inputs:** A set of calibrated images with known camera poses; optionally, an initial sparse point cloud and features derived from a pre-trained multi-view stereo (MVS) network (e.g., MVSNet) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Outputs:** Rendered RGB images, depth maps (optional), and the optimized neural point cloud (positions and latent features) [S1, unofficial].
 
